t.save('45-ar-summary-insufficiency.md','Separate a missing conditional signal model from the choice of what guarantee remains possible with that missing input.','K12 has just established opposite purchase choices for equal-accuracy signals. Consolidation03 retrieves that finding by conditional likelihood, retaining fixed prior/payoff/price. The original objective of a uniformly optimal summary-only choice is now tested alongside a separately declared two-model minimax-regret alternative.','The summary-only selector is deterministic and its choices are buy or act now. A/B likelihoods, price and payoffs are the exact FRQ42 model. Uniform optimality is the criterion for the deep branch. The side branch explicitly changes to a stochastic buy-probability q and worst expected regret over the exhaustive model set {A,B}; that narrower model-set assumption is not claimed for the original source.','R2/R5/R8/R11 reject uniform optimality for a deterministic summary-only selector. R14/R17/R20 show that an additional constant feature does not fix the missing distinction. The R16 expansion gives a different attainable criterion: in the expressly two-model regret problem, q=.5 yields worst expected regret.1. Conditional rates and objective relaxation are different routes, neither an inferred human outcome.','The performed ar45-regret-tests.json compares q=0,.25,.5,.75,1 and selects .5 for the declared worst-regret criterion. Its maximum regret .1 is below the .2 of either fixed action; no claim of zero regret follows. AV50 now receives this randomized two-model policy and must verify the exhaustive-model and linear-expectation assumptions before retaining it.','K13: under a newly explicit two-model minimax-regret objective, the actual model choice changes from either unsupported fixed action to a .5 mixture and halves worst expected regret from.2 to.1. This is a finite decision-model benefit; it neither identifies the signal nor prescribes behavior to the user.','KEEP')
